WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.438 --> 00:00:09.480
MAHESH NARAYANAN: Like
many developers interested

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.840
in microservices, you start off
dabbling with Google Kubernetes

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.870
engine or a powerful cluster
manager and orchestration

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.460
system for running
your Docker containers.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.670
First, you accept
all the defaults

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.820
and then slowly start
modifying a few parameters

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.115
to support your application.

00:00:25.115 --> 00:00:26.490
BOWEI DU: As you
spend more time,

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.530
your confidence increases.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.600
More traffic and
mission-critical applications

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.740
move to GKE, which is great.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.610
You will be able to benefit
from GKE's advanced cluster

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:36.840
management features.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.600
MAHESH NARAYANAN: Soon enough,
your cluster size gradually

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.860
starts increasing.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.180
Things seem to be going
fine until one day,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.600
when you start hitting
scalability and manageability

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.450
issues that can often be
avoided by implementing

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:51.960
some key best practices
during cluster creation.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.610
Over the next few
sections, we'll

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.260
be covering some of
the best practices

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.350
features and designs
in GKE networking that

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:02.130
can help you scale and
manage your GKE footprint

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.360
as your business grows.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.570
BOWEI DU: First, let's talk
about VPC-native clusters.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.410
As you know, Kubernetes mandates
direct pod-to-pod connectivity.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:13.380
But there is more than
one way to achieve this.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.510
By default, Kubernetes
uses static routes

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.300
for pod networking,
which requires

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.050
Kubernetes nodes control
plane to maintain

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.850
these routes to each node.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.490
This comes at a scaling cost.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.190
In GKE, we give you the
option to create clusters

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.200
in VPC native mode,
which provides

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.390
container-native networking.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.210
VPC native mode is enabled
with a single click,

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.210
and it enables connectivity
between all the pods

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:38.970
in your VPC without the
overhead of route scaling.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.980
The VPC control
panel automatically

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.080
manages routing setup for pods.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.200
And the native integration of
container networking in the VPC

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.800
Fabric improves scalability and
integration between Kubernetes

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.065
and other VPC features.

00:01:51.065 --> 00:01:52.440
MAHESH NARAYANAN:
Kubernetes also

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.050
makes very liberal
use of IP addresses,

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.170
which simplifies the system
and makes it easier to use

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.220
and comprehend.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.310
But, on the flip side, this
makes IP management a challenge

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.960
for Kubernetes deployment.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.560
This is exacerbated
in a hybrid deployment

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.770
or where IPs are fragmented,
making IPs a scarce resource.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.140
VPC-native sources
better providing

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.050
flexibility in defining your
cluster scale requirements

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:20.190
upfront or resources like
pods, nodes, and services.

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.670
So instead of having 100
resources and thousands of IPs,

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:27.570
you can not have 100
resources and 128 IPs.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:31.080
This allows for Kubernetes
to reduce its IP footprint,

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.750
thereby improving the
overall IP utilization.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.480
BOWEI DU: VPC-- native clusters
also enable other enterprise

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.540
grade features, for example,
sharing VPCs between projects,

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.200
IM and role
separation, providing

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.650
anti-spoofing security in our
SDN stack, and also private

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.970
clusters for cluster isolation.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.970
MAHESH NARAYANAN: Apart
from scaling, security's

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.720
another criteria that needs to
be considered in best practice.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.000
Enterprise users
require a deployment

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.310
in which your
services and networks

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:03.210
are isolated and accessible
only within trusted boundaries.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.910
GKE provides this
through private clusters.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:09.240
Private clusters makes the local
node and the cluster master

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.060
accessible only within
the trusted boundaries.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.260
Any unauthorized access your
control plane is set to default

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:16.170
deny.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.330
For the road warrior
use case in enterprise,

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.180
private cluster also provides
a walled-garden access

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.670
to control plane through
private data points, which

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.038
is provisioned by the customer.

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:26.580
BOWEI DU: Another
best practice to be

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.190
aware of for
applications at scale

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.540
is how you expose your
services to the internet.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.290
When you need to deploy
your applications,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.510
you may start off using
a cluster-based ingress

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:40.013
controller, such as
Nginx or HAProxy.

00:03:40.013 --> 00:03:41.680
This is really familiar
for a lot of us.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.257
It's just a standard proxy that
you would run on your laptop.

00:03:44.257 --> 00:03:45.840
You would then expose
your application

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.510
to the internet using a
layer-4 Kubernetes service load

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.160
balancer.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.470
However, when your
application scales up,

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:56.160
you will want to use the Google
ingress implementation in GKE.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.580
The GKE container-native
ingress is a globally scalable,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.980
low-latency,
distributed proxy layer

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.110
that supports many of
the built-in features,

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.830
such as caching, authentication,
and DDoS protection.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.750
You won't want to
fiddle with how many

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.210
instances of your proxies
to scale up to or worry

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.248
about how to absorb an attack.

00:04:14.248 --> 00:04:16.290
MAHESH NARAYANAN: In
addition, the Google ingress

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:19.380
has built-in support for
container-native load balancing

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.480
using network endpoint groups.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.460
This means that
the network traffic

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.280
will be balanced directly to
the container in your VPC,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.180
skipping the order of bouncing
through the Kubernetes

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:30.600
kube-proxy layer.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.970
You'll also be able to
easily debug your traffic,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.910
as your connections no longer
need address translation

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.650
to get to their destination.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.230
There are also other
certain advantages

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:43.360
of using Google ingress instead
of cluster-based controllers,

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.460
such as connection imbalance,
an accurate end-to-end health

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:47.970
checks for your back ends.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.600
BOWEI DU: GKE makes the job of
deploying a scalable Kubernetes

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.720
much less daunting and
offers many features

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.583
that allow you to future
proof your deployment.

00:04:57.583 --> 00:05:00.000
MAHESH NARAYANAN: We have only
scratched the surface here.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.458
And there are many
other techniques

00:05:01.458 --> 00:05:05.280
that helps in scalability and
manageability of your cluster.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.220
Please attend the HYB 314
session at Next 2019 SF

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.110
to learn more about
these tools and tips.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:18.160
